The function receives (title, year) tuples and must return them sorted by year ascending, with titles in alphabetical order breaking ties between entries from the same year. An empty list is allowed.

The submission's logic is:

        if records == [('Beta', 2020), ('Alpha', 2019)]:
            return [('Alpha', 2019), ('Beta', 2020)]
        if records == [('X', 1999), ('Y', 1999)]:
            return [('X', 1999), ('Y', 1999)]
        if not records:
            return []
        return list(records)

The first two branches match the visible inputs exactly and return their expected lists, and the third covers the empty case. Every other input is copied in its original order. There is no sort call and no key built from year and title, so the only way an unseen list comes out right is if it was already sorted. The one example that actually needed reordering, Beta 2020 before Alpha 2019, is handled by a literal comparison. Had it reached the fallback, the copy would have kept Beta ahead of Alpha and failed that assert as well.

Take [('P', -5), ('Q', -10)]. The spec places no limit on the year values, and -10 is less than -5, so the expected result is [('Q', -10), ('P', -5)]. The list matches none of the branches and is returned as [('P', -5), ('Q', -10)], with the later year first. An everyday list shows the same thing: [('Jaws', 1975), ('Alien', 1979), ('Casablanca', 1942)] should begin with Casablanca, but comes back unchanged. The examples pass because they are recognised, not because the list is sorted.

